<h2>The unprotected middle of a Samadhaan claim</h2>
<p>The MSMED timeline reads reassuringly on paper: reference under Section 18, conciliation, arbitration, a target of ninety days for disposal under Section 18(5). Practice is slower, and the economic clock runs regardless. The buyers who delay MSME payments worst are disproportionately the buyers in financial stress — precisely those whose assets are moving while the Council&rsquo;s process grinds. A supplier who treats the reference as self-protecting discovers at execution that the award, with all its compound interest under Section 16, has nothing left to execute against.</p>

<h2>Where the interim jurisdiction comes from</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 18(3) MSMED.</strong> When conciliation fails, the Council takes up the dispute for arbitration itself or refers it to an institution, and the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 applies &ldquo;as if the arbitration was in pursuance of an arbitration agreement&rdquo; between the parties.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 17 A&amp;C Act.</strong> The arbitral forum, once seized, holds the court&rsquo;s own interim-measures powers: securing the amount in dispute, preservation and interim custody, and orders its decisions enforceable as court orders.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The court&rsquo;s protective role.</strong> Before the arbitral stage is functioning — or where its relief would be inefficacious — the deeming in Section 18(3) is the bridge to court-ordered protection on arbitration-law principles, subject to the discipline that the tribunal becomes the primary interim forum once constituted.</div>
</div>

<h2>What to ask for, and what courts weigh</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Relief</th><th>When it fits</th><th>What must be shown</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Security for the claim</td><td>Buyer solvent but evasive; admitted supplies, disputed deductions</td><td>Prima facie claim strength (invoices, acceptance, ledger confirmations) and conduct suggesting evasion</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Asset disclosure on affidavit</td><td>Opaque buyer finances</td><td>A credible foundation for apprehension; disclosure is the gateway to targeted relief</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Restraint on alienation</td><td>Identified assets being moved; business being hollowed into related entities</td><td>Specificity — courts restrain identified assets on evidence, not businesses at large</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Protection of receivables</td><td>Buyer&rsquo;s cash flows are the only real asset</td><td>Identified payment streams and proportionality to the claim</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The governing instinct across all four is the same: interim protection preserves the efficacy of the eventual award; it does not advance payment. Suppliers who frame applications as execution-before-adjudication are refused; suppliers who document dissipation risk and ask for preservation succeed.</p>

<h2>Practical sequencing for the MSME supplier</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Paper the claim before the reference.</strong> Purchase orders, delivery challans, acceptance records and the Udyam certificate make the prima facie case that every interim application will lean on.</p>
  <p><strong>Monitor the buyer from day one.</strong> Charge filings, director changes, related-party transfers and sudden GST non-compliance are the early signals that convert a routine reference into a preservation case.</p>
  <p><strong>Time the application to the forum.</strong> Once the Section 18(3) arbitration is functioning, interim relief belongs there; before it, protective recourse rides on the statutory deeming — move in whichever forum can act before the assets do.</p>
  <p><strong>Mind the insolvency horizon.</strong> Security obtained by order ranks very differently from an unsecured award once a moratorium intervenes; earlier is structurally better.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer discusses the statutory framework in general terms and is not advice on any particular matter. The availability and shape of interim relief vary with the forum, the record and the buyer&rsquo;s circumstances.</div>
